1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:03,800
Two minutes.

2
00:00:04,490 --> 00:00:09,300
That is roughly how long Abraham Lincoln
spoke at Gettysburg in 1863,

3
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:12,740
after the main speaker had
talked for about two hours.

4
00:00:13,370 --> 00:00:15,600
Five copies survive in his own hand.

5
00:00:15,970 --> 00:00:19,780
Only one, known as the Bliss copy,
is signed and dated.

6
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:21,840
It is kept in the White House.

7
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:25,020
The man who wrote it had,
by his own count,

8
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:26,800
less than a year of schooling.

9
00:00:37,005 --> 00:00:40,892
Abraham Lincoln was born
on the twelfth of February, 1809,

10
00:00:40,925 --> 00:00:44,695
in a one-room log cabin
on his father's farm in Kentucky.

11
00:00:45,265 --> 00:00:48,515
His parents were Thomas Lincoln,
a carpenter and farmer,

12
00:00:48,825 --> 00:00:49,895
and Nancy Hanks.

13
00:00:50,445 --> 00:00:54,892
When he was seven, the family moved
to Indiana, partly, he later said,

14
00:00:54,925 --> 00:00:58,475
on account of slavery,
but chiefly over land titles.

15
00:00:58,945 --> 00:01:01,575
An axe was put into his hands at once.

16
00:01:06,485 --> 00:01:10,835
When he was nine, his mother died
of what people called milk sickness.

17
00:01:11,525 --> 00:01:15,535
His stepmother, Sarah Bush Johnston,
proved, in his words,

18
00:01:15,745 --> 00:01:17,455
"a good and kind mother".

19
00:01:17,925 --> 00:01:21,072
He wrote of himself,
"What he has in the way of education,

20
00:01:21,105 --> 00:01:22,651
he has picked up."

21
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:30,432
At nineteen,

22
00:01:30,554 --> 00:01:34,824
Lincoln helped take a flatboat down
the Ohio and Mississippi to New Orleans,

23
00:01:35,034 --> 00:01:36,824
over twelve hundred miles.

24
00:01:37,514 --> 00:01:40,281
In 1830 the family moved on to Illinois,

25
00:01:40,314 --> 00:01:43,204
where he split rails to fence ten acres.

26
00:01:43,854 --> 00:01:47,024
Rails like these later became
part of his campaign image.

27
00:01:47,614 --> 00:01:49,184
On a second trip to New Orleans,

28
00:01:49,414 --> 00:01:52,884
he would have seen slavery
on a scale unknown in Illinois.

29
00:01:53,514 --> 00:01:56,724
Stories of its effect on him
came only after his death;

30
00:01:57,194 --> 00:01:59,064
historians treat them with caution.

31
00:01:59,754 --> 00:02:03,704
At New Salem he was a store clerk,
postmaster and surveyor.

32
00:02:03,994 --> 00:02:07,864
In the Black Hawk War, his fellow
volunteers elected him captain.

33
00:02:08,334 --> 00:02:12,244
He saw no battle, but said
nothing later satisfied him more.

34
00:02:16,889 --> 00:02:21,879
In 1834, Lincoln was elected
to the Illinois legislature as a Whig.

35
00:02:22,289 --> 00:02:25,116
He borrowed law books from John T. Stuart,

36
00:02:25,149 --> 00:02:27,719
later his law partner, and taught himself.

37
00:02:28,129 --> 00:02:30,279
"He studied with nobody," he wrote.

38
00:02:30,789 --> 00:02:35,996
In 1837 he moved to Springfield with all
his belongings in two saddlebags.

39
00:02:36,599 --> 00:02:40,179
That year he joined a protest
against slavery in the legislature.

40
00:02:40,749 --> 00:02:42,679
He met Mary Todd at a ball.

41
00:02:44,669 --> 00:02:49,579
Their engagement broke off,
and in January 1841 Lincoln wrote,

42
00:02:50,069 --> 00:02:52,699
"I am now the most miserable man living."

43
00:02:53,349 --> 00:02:56,039
Friends spoke of his bouts
of deep melancholy.

44
00:02:56,549 --> 00:03:01,339
But in November 1842 they married,
and they would have four sons.

45
00:03:02,049 --> 00:03:04,239
He rode the legal circuit for years.

46
00:03:06,289 --> 00:03:10,516
In 1846 he was elected
to Congress for a single term,

47
00:03:10,549 --> 00:03:12,469
where he opposed the war with Mexico

48
00:03:12,809 --> 00:03:16,839
and proposed a bill to begin ending
slavery in the District of Columbia.

49
00:03:21,628 --> 00:03:23,058
In 1854,

50
00:03:23,428 --> 00:03:25,995
the Kansas–Nebraska Act
reopened the question

51
00:03:26,028 --> 00:03:28,118
of slavery in the western territories,

52
00:03:28,388 --> 00:03:32,458
and drew Lincoln back into politics,
in the new Republican Party.

53
00:03:32,888 --> 00:03:38,058
In June 1858, running for the Senate,
he quoted the Bible in a speech.

54
00:03:38,588 --> 00:03:41,628
"A house divided against
itself cannot stand."

55
00:03:42,228 --> 00:03:44,568
"I believe this government cannot endure,

56
00:03:44,868 --> 00:03:47,468
permanently half slave and half free."

57
00:03:48,088 --> 00:03:52,298
He met the Democrat Stephen Douglas
in seven debates across Illinois.

58
00:03:52,688 --> 00:03:55,848
At Charleston he said
something hard to hear today:

59
00:03:56,328 --> 00:04:01,035
"I am not, nor ever have been,
in favor of bringing about in any way

60
00:04:01,068 --> 00:04:05,028
the social and political equality
of the white and black races…"

61
00:04:05,748 --> 00:04:08,798
He also opposed Black
voting and jury service.

62
00:04:09,468 --> 00:04:13,278
The legislature, which chose
senators then, returned Douglas.

63
00:04:13,808 --> 00:04:14,738
Lincoln lost.

64
00:04:15,228 --> 00:04:17,138
But the debates made his name,

65
00:04:17,328 --> 00:04:21,818
and in February 1860 he spoke
at Cooper Union in New York:

66
00:04:22,348 --> 00:04:25,138
"let us have faith
that right makes might".

67
00:04:29,568 --> 00:04:33,978
In May 1860, the Republicans
chose him to run for President.

68
00:04:34,468 --> 00:04:38,018
That October,
an eleven-year-old girl, Grace Bedell,

69
00:04:38,228 --> 00:04:39,838
wrote to him about a beard.

70
00:04:40,448 --> 00:04:42,018
Apparently taking her advice,

71
00:04:42,288 --> 00:04:44,618
he became the first President to wear one.

72
00:04:45,208 --> 00:04:47,998
In November he won a four-way race.

73
00:04:48,528 --> 00:04:51,718
Many white Southerners feared
he would move against slavery.

74
00:04:52,168 --> 00:04:55,678
Before he took office,
seven states had left the Union,

75
00:04:56,048 --> 00:04:57,358
South Carolina first,

76
00:04:57,648 --> 00:05:00,638
and formed the Confederacy
under Jefferson Davis.

77
00:05:01,288 --> 00:05:03,675
Slavery, and the fight over its spread,

78
00:05:03,708 --> 00:05:06,215
lay at the root of the crisis.

79
00:05:06,248 --> 00:05:08,208
His election was the spark.

80
00:05:09,188 --> 00:05:11,438
On the twelfth of April, 1861,

81
00:05:11,808 --> 00:05:15,885
Confederate forces attacked Fort Sumter,
in Charleston harbour.

82
00:05:15,948 --> 00:05:20,618
It surrendered, and Lincoln called
for seventy-five thousand volunteers.

83
00:05:21,088 --> 00:05:22,578
The war had begun.

84
00:05:26,909 --> 00:05:29,199
Early on came defeat at Bull Run.

85
00:05:29,829 --> 00:05:33,625
In February 1862, his son
Willie died, aged eleven.

86
00:05:34,929 --> 00:05:37,059
In August he wrote publicly,

87
00:05:37,569 --> 00:05:40,999
"My paramount object in this
struggle is to save the Union,

88
00:05:41,189 --> 00:05:44,419
and is not either to save
or to destroy slavery."

89
00:05:44,969 --> 00:05:48,519
Yet he had already shown
his Cabinet a draft proclamation.

90
00:05:49,109 --> 00:05:53,039
On the first of January 1863,
as a war measure,

91
00:05:53,289 --> 00:05:56,556
the Emancipation Proclamation
declared that all persons held

92
00:05:56,589 --> 00:05:58,996
as slaves in the rebel states "are,

93
00:05:59,029 --> 00:06:01,159
and henceforward shall be free".

94
00:06:01,549 --> 00:06:04,979
It could not free anyone
beyond the Union army's reach,

95
00:06:05,149 --> 00:06:08,299
and left slavery untouched
in the loyal border states.

96
00:06:08,709 --> 00:06:11,939
But the war was now a war against slavery.

97
00:06:12,269 --> 00:06:15,399
It also opened the army
and navy to Black men;

98
00:06:15,909 --> 00:06:18,259
nearly two hundred thousand served.

99
00:06:18,869 --> 00:06:22,619
Frederick Douglass recruited soldiers,
and two of his sons enlisted.

100
00:06:23,269 --> 00:06:27,569
In July the Union won at Gettysburg,
at terrible cost.

101
00:06:27,969 --> 00:06:28,909
That August,

102
00:06:29,149 --> 00:06:33,179
Frederick Douglass protested to Lincoln
that Black soldiers were paid less.

103
00:06:33,689 --> 00:06:37,039
In November, Lincoln helped
dedicate a cemetery there.

104
00:06:40,749 --> 00:06:42,319
The signed copy begins:

105
00:06:42,969 --> 00:06:47,436
"Four score and seven years ago our
fathers brought forth on this continent,

106
00:06:47,469 --> 00:06:50,379
a new nation, conceived in Liberty,

107
00:06:50,669 --> 00:06:55,071
and dedicated to the proposition
that all men are created equal."

108
00:06:59,161 --> 00:07:01,219
In April 1864,

109
00:07:01,429 --> 00:07:04,619
Lincoln wrote,
"I am naturally anti-slavery.

110
00:07:04,949 --> 00:07:07,809
If slavery is not wrong,
nothing is wrong."

111
00:07:08,429 --> 00:07:10,519
That summer he expected to lose.

112
00:07:11,049 --> 00:07:15,659
Then Union forces took Atlanta,
and in November he was re-elected.

113
00:07:16,389 --> 00:07:20,539
In January 1865, the House
passed the Thirteenth Amendment,

114
00:07:20,789 --> 00:07:22,179
which he had pressed for.

115
00:07:24,449 --> 00:07:27,396
That March,
in his Second Inaugural, he said,

116
00:07:27,429 --> 00:07:31,619
"With malice toward none;
with charity for all;

117
00:07:32,009 --> 00:07:35,719
with firmness in the right,
as God gives us to see the right,

118
00:07:35,849 --> 00:07:38,799
let us strive on to finish
the work we are in;

119
00:07:38,949 --> 00:07:40,969
to bind up the nation's wounds…"

120
00:07:41,729 --> 00:07:45,189
Frederick Douglass recalled
calling it "a sacred effort".

121
00:07:45,509 --> 00:07:48,796
On the ninth of April,
at Appomattox Court House,

122
00:07:48,829 --> 00:07:51,316
Robert E. Lee surrendered
his Confederate army

123
00:07:51,349 --> 00:07:54,349
to the Union commander, Ulysses S. Grant.

124
00:07:54,909 --> 00:07:57,496
An estimated six hundred
and twenty thousand

125
00:07:57,529 --> 00:08:00,579
to seven hundred and fifty
thousand died in the war.

126
00:08:04,949 --> 00:08:08,379
Five days later, on the evening
of the fourteenth of April,

127
00:08:08,809 --> 00:08:12,119
Lincoln was at Ford's Theatre
in Washington, watching a play,

128
00:08:12,329 --> 00:08:13,679
Our American Cousin.

129
00:08:14,129 --> 00:08:17,139
At about a quarter past ten,
John Wilkes Booth,

130
00:08:17,409 --> 00:08:21,916
a Confederate sympathiser and racist
who despised his efforts to end slavery,

131
00:08:21,949 --> 00:08:23,379
shot the President.

132
00:08:24,109 --> 00:08:26,039
Lincoln never regained consciousness.

133
00:08:28,589 --> 00:08:31,639
He was carried across the street
to the Petersen boarding house,

134
00:08:31,889 --> 00:08:35,039
and laid diagonally across
a bed in a small back room,

135
00:08:35,209 --> 00:08:37,379
because he was too tall to lie straight.

136
00:08:38,229 --> 00:08:40,959
Mary waited in the front
parlour with their son Robert.

137
00:08:41,769 --> 00:08:44,479
At twenty-two minutes past
seven the next morning,

138
00:08:44,869 --> 00:08:46,099
Abraham Lincoln died.

139
00:08:46,629 --> 00:08:48,148
He was fifty-six.

140
00:08:53,921 --> 00:08:58,651
His funeral train carried him one
thousand six hundred and fifty-four miles,

141
00:08:58,941 --> 00:09:01,731
through hundreds of towns,
home to Springfield.

142
00:09:02,381 --> 00:09:04,711
His son Willie's coffin
travelled with his.

143
00:09:05,441 --> 00:09:10,531
By December 1865, enough states had
ratified the Thirteenth Amendment.

144
00:09:11,261 --> 00:09:13,751
Slavery was abolished
in the United States.

145
00:09:16,461 --> 00:09:19,971
In 1922 the Lincoln Memorial
was dedicated,

146
00:09:20,101 --> 00:09:24,191
with the Gettysburg Address and the
Second Inaugural carved on its walls.

147
00:09:24,641 --> 00:09:25,891
At the dedication,

148
00:09:26,181 --> 00:09:29,471
Black guests were reportedly
seated in a separate section.

149
00:09:30,161 --> 00:09:32,251
Historians still argue about him.

150
00:09:32,761 --> 00:09:36,671
Some see a man who grew;
others, a man who was pushed.

151
00:09:37,241 --> 00:09:40,011
Frederick Douglass,
who knew him, saw both.

152
00:09:40,381 --> 00:09:42,851
The page he signed ends with a resolve:

153
00:09:43,421 --> 00:09:47,331
"that government of the people,
by the people, for the people,

154
00:09:47,481 --> 00:09:49,241
shall not perish from the earth."

155
00:09:50,021 --> 00:09:54,631
Two minutes, a few sheets of paper,
and an argument that has never ended.
